-- Reading: complex paraphrase, hedging and the writer's view.

For decades, an afternoon nap was regarded as a sign of laziness in many workplaces. Recent research has complicated that view. In one study, employees who slept for twenty minutes after lunch made fewer errors in the late afternoon than colleagues who stayed awake, although the benefit disappeared when naps lasted longer than half an hour. The researchers are careful not to claim that napping suits everyone: people who sleep badly at night may find that daytime sleep makes the problem worse. Nevertheless, a small number of companies now provide quiet rooms where staff can rest, arguing that a short break costs less than the mistakes made by exhausted workers.', 'VALID');
