Supporters of tourism point to the jobs it creates, and on several islands in the region hotels are now the largest employer. Yet the picture is less clear than such figures suggest. Many of the better-paid positions appear to go to workers from the mainland, and a significant share of hotel profits leaves the islands altogether. Some economists argue that tourism tends to push up the price of land, which may make it harder for local families to buy homes. It would be wrong, in my view, to conclude that islands should turn tourists away. The more reasonable lesson is that the benefits of tourism are not automatic: they depend on who owns the hotels and how the income is shared.', 'VALID');
