Sales of plant-based burgers rose quickly in the late 2010s, and some commentators predicted that they would soon replace meat. That has not happened. In several countries sales have levelled off, apparently because many shoppers tried the products once and did not buy them again. Price seems to matter: plant-based burgers usually cost more than beef ones. I find it hard to believe that taste is the only obstacle, since recipes have improved considerably. Producers would probably attract more regular customers by competing on price rather than on novelty.', 'VALID');
